  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 215 PM EDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\nOne last dry day today with a bit of an increase in humidity \nunder shortwave ridging aloft and surface high pressure situated \nover the Appalachians. A fairly stout closed upper low will arrive\nfrom the Northern Plains tomorrow vertically stacked above a \nsurface low. Around Sunday, the trough axis and cut off low \nseemingly parks right over us, for the most part. There's still \nsome uncertainty about how this will play out because of model \ndiscrepancies. It's not until Wednesday that the next trough rolls\nin and absorbs the aforementioned low. Thereafter until the end \nof the forecast period, weak steering develops, and daily chances \nof showers and storms continue with temperatures pushing above 90 \nagain. Precipitation chances later in the week will be best over\nthe higher terrain.\n\nTomorrow will present a chance of strong to possibly a severe \nstorm, as well as isolated flooding. There will be a couple of\nrounds of storms tomorrow with the first between 7 am and 3 pm ET\nand 4 pm until possibly after midnight for the second. Null\nsurface instability amid a low level inversion, will prevent the \nearly day round from getting strong. There may be some \nthunderstorms, but it'll be mostly rain. The area with the\ngreatest threat of stronger storms will be the plateau/west of\nI-40, where SPC has the area under a slight risk. A slim marginal\narea to the east of that and then no severe for much of northeast\nTN and southwest VA. This makes sense as southerly flow over the\nterrain is trying to pull what we'd typically see in winter;\ndownsloping eradicating precipitation or just keeping it at bay.\nThe strongest winds over the higher terrain will be later in the\nday tomorrow, but quickly dropping off into the overnight hours, \nso not like how true winter mountain wave events behave. Southerly\nflow develops in the afternoon and turns out of the SW close to\nmidnight and after, which is why the downsloping shouldn't \npersist overnight. \n\nThe strong to severe threat for later tomorrow  will surely need \nmonitored with CAPE pushing 2000 J/Kg, deep layer shear around 35 \nkt, PWATs nearing 2 inches, and DCAPE over 700. Tornadic activity \nalso cannot be ruled out when surface winds will be at their most \n\"southerly\". 18z HRRR forecast soundings around the plateau area \ndepict southerly or slightly backed winds at the surface, \nincreased SRH, 0-1 shear over 20 kt, and 3CAPE around 70 or \nhigher. This threat will only be limited to western parts of the \nforecast area (plateau), as the entirety of threats is forecast \nto drop off as it moves east, possibly being choked off by the \ndownslope flow. Mainly, damaging wind gusts will be the concern \nwith possible flooding tomorrow.",
